OPNsense Firewall Deployment – Home Lab

Author: Johnson Akpososo Israel
Date: 30 July 2025
Environment: Home Lab
Status: Production-ready (VLAN rollout pending)

Project Overview

This project documents the deployment of a dedicated, open-source firewall using OPNsense 25.1 to replace an ISP-provided router.
The goal was to gain full control over routing, security, and visibility while eliminating vendor lock-in.

The firewall now acts as the core security and networking layer for the home lab, enabling advanced segmentation, monitoring, and future expansion.

Objectives

Replace ISP router with a dedicated firewall

Achieve full visibility into network traffic

Enable VLAN-based network segmentation

Prepare the environment for VPN and IDS/IPS

Avoid proprietary hardware and software lock-in

Platform Chosen

OPNsense 25.1 (amd64)

FreeBSD-based, open-source firewall

Actively maintained by Deciso B.V. and the community

Web-based management interface

Security- and privacy-focused design

Hardware Specifications

Firewall Appliance

Fanless mini-PC

Intel J6412 quad-core CPU

8 GB DDR4 RAM

120 GB SATA SSD

Networking

Dual Intel i226-V NICs

em0: LAN (192.168.1.1/24)

em1: WAN (DHCP client to ISP modem)

Access

USB-C serial console for initial setup and recovery

Installation and Initial Configuration
Installation Media

Downloaded OPNsense 25.1 serial-memstick image

Written to USB using balenaEtcher

System Installation

Booted from USB

Installed using ZFS (single disk) for snapshots and rollback

Interface Assignment

em0 assigned to LAN

em1 configured as WAN (DHCP)

Web Interface

Accessible via https://192.168.1.1

Strong root password configured

Self-signed HTTPS certificate generated (ACME planned)

Security Hardening
SSH

SSH disabled via GUI

Console access retained for emergencies

Web Interface

GUI access restricted to LAN subnet

Anti-lockout rule enabled

System Integrity

SHA256 host keys recorded for verification

Firmware updates pinned to the stable train

Automatic configuration backup enabled before upgrades

Firewall Policy (LAN)

Default LAN → Any rule retained for initial connectivity

Primary allow rule logged for baseline traffic visibility

DNS enforcement:

Allow LAN to firewall on UDP 53

Redirect external DNS requests to Unbound

RFC1918 block rule prepared for future IoT VLAN isolation

Enabled Services
DNS

Unbound DNS resolver in resolver mode

DNSSEC enabled

Time Synchronization

Local NTP server for LAN clients

Intrusion Detection

Suricata enabled with ET-Open rules

Running in IDS (detect-only) mode for tuning

Monitoring

pfTop live traffic view added to dashboard

RRD graphs enabled with 90-day retention

Backup and Monitoring Strategy

Nightly encrypted configuration backups via cron

Offloaded to a private Git repository using git-secret

Manual pre-change backups taken before all modifications

Console recovery options retained

Roadmap

VLAN segmentation

VLAN 20 for IoT devices

VLAN 30 for guest network

WireGuard VPN for remote access

HAProxy for reverse proxying internal services

Transition Suricata to IPS mode after tuning

Network upgrade with 10 GbE NIC and LACP to NAS

Key Takeaways

Deployment completed in under 15 minutes

Web interface remains responsive on low-power hardware

Serial console provides reliable recovery access

Logging revealed hardcoded external DNS usage by LAN clients

Community documentation and forums were effective for troubleshooting




The firewall is production-ready and awaiting the next maintenance window for VLAN deployment.
